1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,973
In this demo we build a new payment scheme from nothing, and connect it to the corporate payment workflow. The scheme is called U A E demo. It is synthetic, made up for training, and its file is a simple list: a header record, then one record per credit.

2
00:00:14,581 --> 00:00:23,796
First, the test data. In the test bench, we save three synthetic sample files: a valid file, a file in dollars, and a file that is not a demo file at all.

3
00:00:29,604 --> 00:00:39,182
Next, the port: the connection to the demo clearing house. Every port needs a simulator that stands in for the party in tests, so we write the port, and save it as a draft.

4
00:00:39,791 --> 00:00:49,177
The simulator plays the clearing house. It carries the eight standard scenarios: accept, reject, no reply, a late reply, duplicates, and more. We save it too.

5
00:00:49,779 --> 00:01:01,875
The port and its simulator refer to each other, so neither can be published first. The studio generates a change set: approving one publishes both, checked and tested together. A checker who is not the maker approves it.

6
00:01:02,483 --> 00:01:11,592
Now the rule: a demo file must be in dirhams, otherwise it is rejected with reason code A M zero three. Its two examples run in the gates as we edit.

7
00:01:12,194 --> 00:01:19,532
The rule goes into a rule set, and the rule set into the file checks: a validate activity, with the outcomes pass and reject.

8
00:01:28,251 --> 00:01:36,805
The intake map reads the file into the canonical context. It is bound to a script, so once the draft is saved, a script-behind editor appears.

9
00:01:37,408 --> 00:01:50,506
The script reads the header and the credits, and writes the batch and its items. It uses only what the studio injects: no clock, no randomness, no network. It does not say what payment the file starts. The dispatch table does that.

10
00:01:51,112 --> 00:02:00,967
Three more blocks: a map that renders the file for the clearing house, a map that reads its acknowledgement, and the send itself, which may be retried, so it carries an idempotency key.

11
00:02:15,254 --> 00:02:24,896
Submitting the file and waiting for the acknowledgement is one composite step. Its outcomes are accepted, rejected, and pending if no answer comes within a business day.

12
00:02:25,499 --> 00:02:32,347
Underneath, a subflow does the work: send the file, await the acknowledgement, and read it. We build it in the workflow designer.

13
00:02:34,469 --> 00:02:45,583
Now the leg: the scheme part of the payment. It sets the business date, checks the file, checks every IBAN, renders the file and submits it. It fills the scheme leg slot of the corporate payment workflow.

14
00:02:46,185 --> 00:02:57,641
Last, the scheme and its dispatch table. The credit file starts an outward corporate payment, read by our intake map, with our leg in the slot. The acknowledgement is delivered to the workflow that awaits it.

15
00:02:58,245 --> 00:03:06,181
These four refer to one another, so the change set holds them all: the scheme, the composite, the subflow and the leg. The checker approves them together.

16
00:03:06,784 --> 00:03:15,082
Let us run the leg on the bench, against the simulator. The file is checked, both IBANs pass, the file is submitted and acknowledged, and the leg settles.

17
00:03:20,554 --> 00:03:32,671
Now we act as the example bank. A bank chooses which rails it runs. Until it accepts the new scheme and enables its rail, a payment in it is rejected at the route. So we release a new configuration set.

18
00:03:33,279 --> 00:03:45,204
And now a whole payment. The credit file arrives from the bank channel. The dispatch table routes it to the corporate workflow, which screens it, reserves the funds, chooses our rail, runs our leg in its slot, and posts it.

19
00:03:58,470 --> 00:04:09,819
Time to export. We export the corporate workflow for the bank, for D T F X, with simulators for a test deployment. The new scheme gets a worker of its own, beside the platform, the cluster and the bank.

20
00:04:10,422 --> 00:04:22,070
Finally, we deploy the package on D T F X. Each worker starts as its own process. The front door reads the file, the dispatch table routes it, and the payment settles on a real workflow engine.

21
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:34,946
That is a scheme built from scratch, routed by its dispatch table into the corporate payment workflow, approved through maker and checker, enabled by a bank, and run on a real engine. The user guide walks through every step.
